        You agree that that’s pretty rough. But hey, his dad is just looking out for him. He may be a bit of a hard ass, but at least <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> knows he cares. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Well, I wouldn’t mind if he cared a little less.”</span> He sighs, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Maybe if I complain about feeling light-headed, mom will make him give me a break…”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> ponders to himself out loud, <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“No, that will probably end badly.”</span> You lean back next to <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span>, moving to pat him on the shoulder comfortingly, but pull away as he groans in pain. <br>
        <br>
        <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Hey, are you better at this talking to adults stuff?”</span> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> turns to you with a desperate, yet not hopeful, smile. <span style="Color: LightSteelBlue">“Got any advice?”</span> <span style="Color: Yellow">Not expecting him to ask you, you ponder for a moment.</span> <br>
        <br>
        <div id="choice2">
            <<link "Tell him to just try his best<br>">>
            <<replace"#choice2">>
                You tell <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> that the best thing he can do is just try his best. His dad is doing this for his own good and it may be for the best for <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> to toughen up a little. The best advice you can give him is to try his best and maybe if his dad starts to see some progress, he’ll lighten up. <span style="Color: LightCoral"> <span style="color:DarkTurquoise">Ben</span> groans loudly at this, your advice being exactly what he didn’t want to hear.</span>
        
                <<set _ad to [
